    <section class="introduction">
      <div>
        <h2 id="introduction">1. Introduction</h2>
        <div class="pre-introduction-image">
          <img src={bannerImage.src} alt="Amazon river and forest." />
        </div>
        <figure>
          <img src="/projects/br163-deforestation-2000-2019.webp" alt="Animated satellite imagery showing deforestation along BR-163 from 2000 to 2019." />
          <figcaption>BR-163 deforestation, 2000–2019.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>In recent decades, deforestation has become a central international concern because of its increasingly perceptible effects on climate, biodiversity, and human wellbeing. The issue is especially significant in Brazil, which, together with Bolivia, Colombia, Ecuador, Guyana, Peru, Suriname, Venezuela, and French Guiana, contains the Amazon—the world’s largest rainforest.</p>
        <p>According to the Amazon Institute of People and the Environment (Imazon), the Amazon recorded alarming levels of deforestation and forest degradation in 2024 <a class="citation" href="#ref-1">[1]</a>.</p>
        <p>The most recent data reveal a critical situation. In October 2024, the Amazon registered its fifth consecutive month of growth in deforestation and degradation, the latter intensified by fires and logging. In that month alone, 6,623 km² were degraded, equivalent to four times the city of São Paulo. From January to October 2024, degradation reached 32,869 km², equivalent to 21 times São Paulo—or about 10,000 football fields per day. This was the worst situation in 15 years and almost three times the previous historical record, 11,453 km² in 2017. Although only about 10% of the accumulated total represents deforested areas, it remains a critical concern because it opens affected regions to exploitation <a class="citation" href="#ref-2">[2]</a>.</p>
        <div class="intro-evidence">
          <figure><img src={imazonOctoberChart.src} alt="Imazon chart of October forest degradation from 2009 to 2024." /><figcaption>October degradation series.</figcaption></figure>
          <figure><img src={imazonYearChart.src} alt="Imazon chart of forest degradation from January to October, 2009 to 2024." /><figcaption>January–October degradation cumulative series.</figcaption></figure>
        </div>
        <p>This scenario demonstrates the urgent need to confront the challenges of preserving the Amazon, one of the world’s richest and most essential ecosystems. It is fundamental to global biodiversity, mitigates climate change, and sustains the ways of life of Indigenous and traditional communities.</p>
        <figure class="intro-image">
          <img src={amazonIndigenous.src} alt="Indigenous child in the Amazon." />
        </figure>
        <p>Compared with other forms of action, forecasting plays a crucial role in preserving the Amazon. The ability to predict where and when forest degradation will occur allows authorities and conservation organisations to act preventively, allocate resources more efficiently, and direct inspection efforts to the most threatened areas. In addition, forecasting can facilitate more effective public policies and mitigation measures before destruction causes irreparable damage. Anticipating deforestation events is therefore an indispensable strategic tool in the fight against environmental degradation.</p>
        <p>In this context, the proposed work develops and evaluates a deep-learning model for forecasting deforestation areas, making it possible to anticipate and more effectively combat adverse impacts on this vital ecosystem. It treats the problem as video prediction: spatial-temporal data generate maps forecasting risk hotspots, placing the task in a form widely studied by the academic community.</p>
        <p>The adopted model is SimVPv2, an improved version of SimVP <a class="citation" href="#ref-3">[3]</a>, proposed as a simple yet powerful approach to spatiotemporal predictive learning <a class="citation" href="#ref-4">[4]</a>. It addresses limitations of neural networks for video prediction, including computational efficiency and the accurate modelling of spatiotemporal patterns. This is the central problem of this work.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

