    <section>
      <h2 id="current-situation">2. Current Situation</h2>
      <p>The growing devastation of the Amazon, as described in the previous section, requires solutions that combine speed, precision, and scalability in monitoring and forecasting risk areas. Several detection and alert systems have been developed to meet this need; one of the most widely used is DETER (Near Real-Time Deforestation Detection), from the National Institute for Space Research (INPE) <a class="citation" href="#ref-5">[5]</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h3 id="deter-data">2.1 DETER Data</h3>
      <p>DETER is an INPE near-real-time system that monitors and identifies deforested or degraded forest areas in the Amazon from satellite imagery. Its MODIS sensor aboard the Terra satellite provides daily updates, but its 250-metre spatial resolution limits detection of smaller areas and detailed degradation patterns <a class="citation" href="#ref-5">[5]</a>.</p>
      <p>This work uses data available through TerraBrasilis, including degradation and logging alerts in the Legal Amazon since 2016 <a class="citation" href="#ref-6">[6]</a>. TerraBrasilis is an INPE web platform for access, consultation, analysis, and dissemination of spatial data generated by governmental environmental-monitoring programmes such as PRODES and DETER. These data are based on imagery from satellites such as Landsat, enabling the mapping and quantification of areas alerted by DETER <a class="citation" href="#ref-7">[7]</a>.</p>
      <p>DETER data are supplied as shapefiles (<code>*.shp</code>), a format widely used in geographic information systems. They contain spatial information represented by polygons delimiting alerted deforestation areas. Each polygon is associated with tabular attributes, including alert date, extent of deforested area, and land-use category. QGIS enables the data to be viewed georeferentially, facilitating spatial analysis and visual interpretation.</p>
    </section>

