    <section>
      <h3 id="simvpv2">2.2 SimVPv2</h3>
      <p>Deep-learning models play a fundamental role in forecasting tasks, particularly image and video analysis. ResUNet <a class="citation" href="#ref-8">[8]</a>, DeepLabV3 <a class="citation" href="#ref-9">[9]</a>, and Swin U-Net <a class="citation" href="#ref-10">[10]</a> are effective for segmentation and modelling complex high-dimensional patterns: ResUNet combines residual networks with U-Net; DeepLabV3 uses dilated convolutions to capture broad context; and Swin U-Net applies transformers to model global spatial relationships. This work implements SimVPv2 because it captures spatiotemporal interactions in video sequences and is therefore suited to deforestation forecasting <a class="citation" href="#ref-4">[4]</a>.</p>
      <h4 id="formal-definition">2.2.1 Formal Definition</h4>
      <p>Following the formal definition of SimVPv2 for a spatiotemporal predictive-learning problem, given a video sequence <math><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></math> at time <math><mi>t</mi></math>, with <math><mi>T</mi></math> past frames, the objective is to predict the next <math><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></math> frames <math><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></math> from time <math><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math>, where <math><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></math> is generally an image with <math><mi>C</mi></math> channels, height <math><mi>H</mi></math>, and width <math><mi>W</mi></math>. In practice, observed input sequences and predicted sequences are represented as tensors: <math><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></math> and <math><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>×</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></math>.</p>
      <math display="block" aria-label="F subscript theta maps X subscript t T to Y subscript t plus 1 T prime"><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>Θ</mi></msub><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></math>
      <p>The model learns this mapping, with parameters <math><mi>Θ</mi></math>, by exploring both spatial and temporal dependencies. In SimVP, <math><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>Θ</mi></msub></math> is a neural-network model trained to minimise the difference between predicted future frames and ground-truth future frames. The optimal parameters are:</p>
      <math display="block" aria-label="theta star equals arg min theta of L of F theta X t T comma Y t plus 1 T prime"><msup><mi>Θ</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><munder><mi>arg min</mi><mi>Θ</mi></munder><mi>L</mi><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>Θ</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></math>
      <p>Here, <math><mi>L</mi></math> is a loss function that evaluates these differences.</p>
      <h4>2.2.2 Model Layers</h4>
      <p>This mapping is performed by convolutional layers organised into three principal components: Input/Output, Spatial Encoder/Decoder, and Translator.</p>
    </section>

    <figure>
      <img src={simvpFramework.src} alt="SimVPv2 framework with spatial encoder, translator, and spatial decoder." />
      <figcaption>SimVPv2 framework <a class="citation" href="#ref-4">[4]</a>.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section>
      <h4>Input/Output</h4>
      <p>The model receives a five-dimensional tensor, <math><mi>B</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>W</mi></math>, corresponding to a sequence of video frames. <math><mi>B</mi></math> is a batch of samples; <math><mi>T</mi></math> is the sequence of frame times; <math><mi>C</mi></math> denotes image channels; and <math><mi>H</mi></math> and <math><mi>W</mi></math> are frame height and width. These dimensions encompass the information needed for temporal and spatial video processing. The model ultimately outputs a five-dimensional tensor of the same form, representing the predicted video sequence.</p>
      <h4>Spatial Encoder/Decoder</h4>
      <p>The spatial encoder transforms the originally high-dimensional past frames into a low-dimensional latent space. For the encoder and decoder, input tensors are reshaped to <math><mo>(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>×</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>W</mi></math>. This allows each frame to be treated independently as a sample, concentrating processing on spatial features without considering temporal variation.</p>
    </section>

    <figure>
      <img src={simvpStructure.src} alt="Tensor dimensionality structure of SimVPv2." />
      <figcaption>SimVPv2 tensor dimensionality structure <a class="citation" href="#ref-4">[4]</a>.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section>
      <h4>Encoder</h4>
      <p>The spatial encoder uses <math><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></math> simple convolutional layers (Conv2d in PyTorch), while the spatial decoder uses <math><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></math> upsampling layers (PixelShuffle in PyTorch). The spatial encoder operation <math><mi>f</mi></math> is:</p>
      <math display="block" aria-label="z sub i equals sigma of Norm2d of Conv2d of z sub i minus 1"><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>(</mo><mtext>Norm2d</mtext><mo>(</mo><mtext>Conv2d</mtext><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo><mo>)</mo><mo>)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width="0.5em"/><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></math>
      <p>Here, <math><mi>σ</mi></math> is a nonlinear activation function; <math><mtext>Norm2d</mtext></math> is a normalisation layer; and <math><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math> is the input tensor, <math><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>×</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></math>. Convolutional layers have stride one except for downsampling layers, whose stride is two. Downsampling is applied once every two convolutional layers.</p>
      <h4>Decoder</h4>
      <p>In the spatial decoder <math><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>, latent representations are reconstructed as:</p>
      <math display="block" aria-label="z sub k equals sigma of Norm2d of unConv2d of z sub k minus 1"><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>(</mo><mtext>Norm2d</mtext><mo>(</mo><mtext>unConv2d</mtext><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo><mo>)</mo><mo>)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width="0.5em"/><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></math>
      <p>Here, <math><mi>σ</mi></math> is a nonlinear activation function, <math><mtext>Norm2d</mtext></math> is a normalisation layer, and <math><mtext>unConv2d</mtext></math> is an upsampling convolutional layer. As in the encoder, upsampling occurs once every two convolutional layers. This approach enables the spatial encoder and decoder to capture and reconstruct the spatial details of each frame efficiently while maintaining a simple, direct structure.</p>
      <h4>Translator</h4>
      <p>The intermediate component, called the spatiotemporal translator, captures both spatial dependencies and temporal variation in latent space. It consists of <math><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></math> temporal modules, whose hidden representations are:</p>
      <math display="block" aria-label="z sub j equals TemporalModule of z sub i minus 1"><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mtext>TemporalModule</mtext><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width="0.5em"/><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></math>
      <p>Here, <math><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math> is the spatial encoder output. These hidden representations are reshaped to <math><mi>B</mi><mo>×</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>W</mi></math>, organising features from multiple frames along the temporal axis. This forces the temporal module, designed with convolutional networks, to learn from stacks of features from multiple frames; it can therefore capture the intrinsic temporal evolution in sequential data.</p>
      <h4>Gated Spatiotemporal Attention Module</h4>
      <p>The Gated Spatiotemporal Attention Module comprises depth-wise convolution, dilated depth-wise convolution, and channel-wise convolution. They respectively capture local receptive fields within channels, establish long-range spatial-temporal connections, and integrate extracted features across channels.</p>
    </section>

    <figure>
      <img src={simvpGsta.src} alt="Gated Spatiotemporal Attention Module architecture." />
      <figcaption>Gated Spatiotemporal Attention Module of SimVPv2 <a class="citation" href="#ref-4">[4]</a>.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section>
      <h4>Mapping Function</h4>
      <p>This combination of convolutions allows the model to integrate spatial and temporal information efficiently and comprehensively.</p>
      <p>The mapping function is the composition of the model components:</p>
      <math display="block" aria-label="F theta equals f inverse composition h composition f"><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>Θ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∘</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>∘</mo><mi>f</mi></math>
      <p>Here, <math><mi>f</mi></math> is the spatial encoder, <math><mi>h</mi></math> is the spatiotemporal translator, <math><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> is the spatial decoder, and <math><mo>∘</mo></math> denotes function composition. A residual connection between the first encoder layer and the final decoder layer preserves spatial features.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="proposal">3. Proposal</h2>
      <p>This work proposes integrating DETER-system data with the SimVP predictive model to create a solution capable of forecasting temporal patterns in spatial data. Its principal objective is to use DETER information together with SimVP’s efficiency and simplicity to produce forecasts that assist environmental monitoring, including deforestation and other land-use changes.</p>
      <h3 id="data-transformation">3.1 Data Transformation</h3>
      <p>To achieve this objective, the first step is to transform DETER data into a format resembling a video sequence. The following steps are used:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Regular-grid creation:</strong> using the legal boundaries of the Amazon, a 1 km² grid is generated, where each square is treated as a pixel.</li>
        <li><strong>Coordinate-system conversion:</strong> DETER data are converted from EPSG:4674 (degrees) to EPSG:5880 (metres), providing greater spatial precision and grid uniformity.</li>
        <li><strong>Temporal grouping:</strong> data are organised into biweekly frames; polygon spatial features are dissolved and consolidated inside their respective pixel and time frame.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The DETER data are then organised into channels so that the model can capture different aspects of deforestation information. Alert polygons are segmented and evaluated within each grid pixel, producing the following channels:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Deforestation history:</strong> past deforestation values over the previous eight periods, providing a temporal view of alert progression.</li>
        <li><strong>Normalised deforestation-polygon count:</strong> the number of polygons detected in each period, normalised for consistent comparison.</li>
        <li><strong>Cumulative deforestation-alert area:</strong> the sum of alerted areas in preceding periods.</li>
        <li><strong>Alerted-area difference series:</strong> variation in deforestation area between consecutive periods.</li>
        <li><strong>Moving-average seasonality factor:</strong> seasonal patterns that influence deforestation.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>These layers provide a rich, detailed base of structured data, enabling more accurate analysis and prediction of spatial and temporal deforestation change.</p>
      <h3 id="patches">3.2 Patches</h3>
      <p>The overall Amazon image is segmented into patches with the following characteristics: height <math><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></math> pixels on the Y axis; width <math><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></math> pixels on the X axis; five channels <math><mi>C</mi></math>; and eight periods <math><mi>T</mi></math>. These patches are SimVP inputs, providing a rich, detailed structure that enables the model to capture both spatial and temporal dependencies. This approach also incorporates seasonality and historical deforestation patterns, enriching predictive capability.</p>
      <p>By transforming DETER data into a structured sequence of temporal frames, each pixel represents a spatial unit while each temporal frame reflects the data state over a biweekly interval. This organisation is fundamental to feeding SimVP, which operates efficiently with inputs formatted like videos.</p>
    </section>

