    <section>
      <h3 id="model-modifications">3.3 Model Modifications</h3>
      <p>Finally, SimVP must be modified so that its sequential output generates a single image. This changes the model mapping to <math><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>Θ</mi></msub><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math>, where <math><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>}</mo><mn>1</mn></math>. Two measures are required:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Output-dimension change:</strong> the current output <math><mi>B</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>W</mi></math> is adjusted to <math><mi>B</mi><mo>×</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>W</mi></math>, combining the temporal and channel dimensions.</li>
        <li><strong>Regression-layer addition:</strong> a regression head implemented with a fully connected network applies a <math><mn>1</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn></math> convolution, mapping the combined <math><mi>T</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>C</mi></math> layers to one output channel.</li>
      </ol>
      <math display="block" aria-label="B times T times C times H times W maps to B times T times C times H times W, then to B times 1 times H times W">
        <mi>B</mi><mo>×</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>W</mi>
      </math>
      <p>A regression head, implemented as a fully connected 1 × 1 convolution, maps the combined <math><mi>T</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>C</mi></math> layers to a single output channel:</p>
      <math display="block" aria-label="Y equals Conv2D of T times C with kernel one">
        <mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>Conv2D</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>kernel</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo>
      </math>
      <p>These changes allow SimVP to produce a single consolidated image as output while preserving the integrity of information extracted from the input sequences.</p>
    </section>

    <figure>
      <img src={simvpOutputModification.src} alt="Diagram of the modified SimVP pipeline, from dataset input through encoder, translator, decoder, and regression head to one output." />
      <figcaption>Modification of SimVP output with a regression head.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section>
      <h3 id="loss-function">3.4 Loss Function</h3>
      <p>Weighted mean squared error was selected for deforestation-area prediction because it is necessary to emphasise deforested regions. Most of the map shows no change, which could unbalance the model by favouring null predictions that minimise mean error. WMSE addresses this problem by penalising errors in critical areas more severely, ensuring relevant patterns are captured more accurately without empty areas exerting disproportionate influence:</p>
      <math display="block" aria-label="Weighted MSE equals the sum of w sub i times y sub i minus y hat sub i squared, divided by the sum of w sub i">
        <mtext>WMSE</mtext><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo><msup><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac>
      </math>
      <p>Here, <math><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math> is the actual value, <math><msub><mover><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></math> the predicted value, <math><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math> the contextual-mask weight associated with the <math><mi>i</mi></math>-th datum, and <math><mi>n</mi></math> the total number of samples. In this case, standard MSE is calculated without reduction; a conditional mask assigns greater weights to deforestation regions, and weighted errors are normalised by the sum of their weights. This directs learning towards areas of greatest importance despite a dataset dominated by unchanged areas.</p>
      <p>WMSE is a variation of MSE that uses adaptive weights based on contextual data to prioritise the most relevant regions. This approach enables the model to capture deforestation patterns in the regions of interest more precisely, even where most data correspond to areas without change. WMSE makes learning robust and directed toward the most important regions.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="results">4. Results</h2>
      <h3 id="visualizations">4.1 Georeferenced Visualizations</h3>
      <p>To validate the proposed model, experiments were conducted with DETER data processed in patches. Results are analysed from performance metrics and georeferenced visualisations.</p>
      <p>Georeferenced visualisations are fundamental to spatial-data analysis because they make patterns and variation intuitive to understand. Heatmaps compare real data (ground truth) with model predictions by translating numerical information into intensity representations, facilitating identification of agreement and discrepancy between predicted and actual values.</p>
      <p>Heatmaps are generated from values associated with each georeferenced pixel, representing predicted and actual deforestation-area intensities. This supports model validation and provides insight into its effectiveness at capturing spatial and temporal patterns in different regions, while allowing critical areas to be visually explored.</p>
      <p>In the heatmaps below, high-deforestation points in the actual data correspond to intensity peaks in predicted areas. These areas of greater concentration represent the most significant deforestation regions; values gradually decrease around them, producing a smooth transition. This is essential evidence that the model identifies critical regions and captures their nearby spatial context.</p>
    </section>

    <figure>
      <img src={heatmap.src} alt="Comparison of observed deforestation alerts and the SimVPv2 prediction heatmap." />
      <figcaption>Deforestation hotspots: label and prediction.</figcaption>
    </figure>

