    <section>
      <h3 id="error-measures">4.2 Error Measures</h3>
      <p>Quantitative evaluation uses RMSE (root mean square error) and MAE (mean absolute error), both widely used to measure forecasting-model accuracy.</p>
      <h4>RMSE</h4>
      <p>RMSE measures the mean squared error between predictions and actual values, then takes its square root:</p>
      <math display="block" aria-label="RMSE equals the square root of one over n times the sum of y sub i minus y hat sub i squared">
        <mtext>RMSE</mtext><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo><msup><mn>2</mn></msup></msqrt>
      </math>
      <p>In this expression, <math><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math> is the actual value, <math><msub><mover><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></math> is the predicted value, and <math><mi>n</mi></math> is the total number of samples. RMSE is sensitive to large errors because squaring penalises larger deviations more severely; this makes it useful for identifying models that minimise major discrepancies, although it can be influenced by outliers.</p>
      <h4>MAE</h4>
      <p>MAE measures the mean absolute error between predicted and actual values:</p>
      <math display="block" aria-label="MAE equals one over n times the sum of the absolute value of y sub i minus y hat sub i">
        <mtext>MAE</mtext><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>|</mo>
      </math>
      <p>Here, <math><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math>, <math><msub><mover><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></math>, and <math><mi>n</mi></math> have the same meanings. MAE is less sensitive to outliers because it uses absolute rather than squared error, giving a more direct interpretation of mean error in the data’s units.</p>
      <h3 id="model-evaluation">4.3 Model Evaluation</h3>
      <p>The charts show SimVPv2 RMSE and MAE across biweekly intervals. RMSE starts near 0.026, peaks close to 0.027, and falls to about 0.023. The increase in the tenth fortnight coincides with the start of the dry season (the Amazonian winter), when validation data show a significant rise in deforested area and the model faces a more difficult forecasting problem. Despite these increases, the overall value remains relatively low, suggesting acceptable performance in capturing the principal data characteristics.</p>
      <p>MAE follows a pattern similar to RMSE, increasing in the same critical periods but remaining slightly lower because it is less sensitive to large discrepancies. Its values range from 0.019 to 0.021, with peaks aligned with periods of greater deforestation activity. This relationship shows that the model faces challenges during high-impact seasonal periods but remains consistent in forecasting general patterns.</p>
      <p>These results underscore the robustness of the model and indicate opportunities for adjustments that account for seasonal factors and abrupt changes in deforestation.</p>
    </section>

    <figure>
      <img src={simvpErrors.src} alt="SimVPv2 RMSE and MAE values across biweekly evaluation periods." />
      <figcaption>SimVPv2 error variation across biweekly periods.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section>
      <h3 id="model-comparison">4.4 Model Comparison</h3>
      <p>All evaluated models—DeepLabV3+, ResUNet, Swin-UNet, and SimVPv2—follow the data’s seasonal trend, with error peaks during the Amazon dry season. This reflects the greater complexity of forecasting critical periods, when deforestation rates rise substantially. SimVPv2 has slightly higher error than the other models, partly because dataset adjustments produced less informative training data. Nevertheless, it remains competitive not only in error metrics but particularly in the quality of the generated visualisations, which clearly and in detail represent relevant spatial patterns.</p>
    </section>

    <figure>
      <img src={modelErrorComparison.src} alt="RMSE and MAE comparison of DeepLabV3+, ResUNet, Swin-UNet, and SimVPv2." />
      <figcaption>Error comparison with DeepLabV3+, ResUNet, and Swin-UNet.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section>
      <h2 id="conclusion">5. Conclusion</h2>
      <p>This work implemented and analysed SimVPv2 for forecasting deforestation areas using spatiotemporal data. Its performance was compared with other models, including DeepLabV3+, ResUNet, and Swin-UNet, demonstrating its competitiveness both in error metrics and in the quality of generated forecasts. This analysis highlighted SimVPv2’s potential as an effective environmental-monitoring tool, even given the challenges imposed by the problem’s complexity.</p>
      <p>Given the scope of the topic, several possibilities remain for improving and adjusting the model. Performance can be improved through fine-tuning, optimising hyperparameters such as learning rate and number of layers. These adjustments can increase the model’s ability to capture complex patterns and subtler dynamics present in the data.</p>
      <p>As a continuation for future work, the dataset can be expanded by including additional variables, such as climatic and socioeconomic data, which have a significant effect on deforestation patterns. In addition, exploring mobile devices for field-data collection could further enrich the information base.</p>
      <p>Finally, training on machines with greater computational power would enable larger, more robust models capable of handling the scale and complexity of the problem, producing more accurate and comprehensive forecasts. The expectation is not only to increase forecasting accuracy but also to advance the practical application of deep-learning models in monitoring and combating deforestation, contributing to the sustainable management of natural resources.</p>
    </section>

